{"result": {"id": "chatcmpl-CyCIohomW8z7j4LMWokkZCCfgx061", "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "logprobs": null, "message": {"content": "{\n    \"title\": \"Analysis of Variance Community\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community centers around the Analysis of Variance (ANOVA), a statistical method used to compare means among different groups. Key entities include the F-Value, Block Totals, and various statistical tests and principles that support ANOVA. These entities are interconnected through their roles in statistical analysis, providing a comprehensive framework for understanding variance in experimental data.\",\n    \"rating\": 8.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is high due to the critical role of ANOVA in various fields, including agriculture, medicine, and social sciences, where it informs decision-making based on statistical evidence.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Core Functionality of ANOVA\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) is a fundamental statistical method that allows researchers to determine if there are significant differences between the means of three or more independent groups. This method is essential in experimental design, as it helps in assessing the impact of one or more factors on a response variable by comparing variances within and between groups. The F-Value, a key statistic derived from ANOVA, quantifies the ratio of variance explained by the model to the variance unexplained, thus serving as a critical tool for hypothesis testing. [Data: Entities (4, 239); Relationships (249)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Importance of the F-Value\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The F-Value is a crucial component of ANOVA, representing the ratio of the variance between groups to the variance within groups. A higher F-Value indicates a greater disparity between group means, suggesting that treatment effects may be significant. The calculation of the F-Value involves determining the mean squares for both between-group and within-group variances, which are essential for conducting ANOVA. This statistic is pivotal in determining whether to reject the null hypothesis, making it a cornerstone of statistical analysis. [Data: Entities (239); Relationships (249)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Block Totals in ANOVA\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Block Totals are utilized in ANOVA to assess the effects of treatments while controlling for variability associated with blocking factors. By summarizing data within blocks, researchers can more accurately compare treatment effects across different experimental conditions. This approach enhances the precision of treatment effect estimates and is particularly useful in designs where variability needs to be accounted for, such as randomized block designs. [Data: Entities (256); Relationships (310)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Applications of ANOVA in Various Fields\",\n            \"explanation\": \"ANOVA is widely applied across multiple disciplines, including agriculture, medicine, and social sciences. In agriculture, it is used to compare crop yields under different treatments, while in medicine, it assesses the effectiveness of various drugs. In social sciences, ANOVA helps evaluate differences in survey responses across demographic groups. The versatility of ANOVA makes it a fundamental tool for researchers seeking to draw meaningful conclusions from experimental data. [Data: Relationships (249)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Limitations and Assumptions of ANOVA\",\n            \"explanation\": \"While ANOVA is a powerful statistical tool, it is sensitive to violations of its assumptions, particularly normality and homogeneity of variances. If these assumptions are not met, the results may be misleading. Additionally, ANOVA does not indicate which specific groups are different; post-hoc tests are required for that purpose. Researchers must ensure that the assumptions are checked and met before conducting ANOVA to maintain the integrity of their findings. [Data: Relationships (249)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Post-hoc Tests Following ANOVA\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Post-hoc tests, such as Tukey’s test, are often employed after ANOVA to further investigate which treatment means are significantly different. These tests are essential for making pairwise comparisons among group means while controlling for Type I error. The use of post-hoc tests enhances the robustness of the analysis and provides clearer insights into the data, allowing researchers to draw more precise conclusions about treatment effects. [Data: Entities (267); Relationships (1086)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Non-parametric Alternatives to ANOVA\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Kruskal-Wallis test serves as a non-parametric alternative to ANOVA, particularly when the assumptions of normality are not met. This test allows researchers to compare the medians of two or more independent groups without relying on the same assumptions as ANOVA. The availability of non-parametric alternatives ensures that researchers have robust methods for analyzing data, even when traditional parametric methods are inappropriate. [Data: Entities (1042); Relationships (1267)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"The Gauss-Markov Theorem and ANOVA\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Gauss-Markov theorem provides foundational principles that are applied in the Analysis of Variance methodology. It establishes the conditions under which the ordinary least squares (OLS) estimator is the best linear unbiased estimator (BLUE) of the parameters in a linear model. This theorem underpins many statistical methods, including ANOVA, by ensuring that the estimators used are efficient and unbiased, thus enhancing the reliability of statistical conclusions drawn from experimental data. [Data: Entities (150); Relationships (154)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": [], "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null}, "content_filter_results": {"hate": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "protected_material_code": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "protected_material_text": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "self_harm": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "sexual": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "violence": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}}}], "created": 1768463006, "model": "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18", "object": "chat.completion", "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": "fp_f97eff32c5", "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1118, "prompt_tokens": 10990, "total_tokens": 12108, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": 0, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cached_tokens": 0}}, "prompt_filter_results": [{"prompt_index": 0, "content_filter_results": {"hate": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "jailbreak": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "self_harm": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "sexual": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "violence": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}}}]}, "input": {"messages": [{"content": "\nYou are an AI assistant that helps a human analyst to perform general information discovery. Information discovery is the process of identifying and assessing relevant information associated with certain entities (e.g., organizations and individuals) within a network.\n\n# Goal\nWrite a comprehensive report of a community, given a list of entities that belong to the community as well as their relationships and optional associated claims. The report will be used to inform decision-makers about information associated with the community and their potential impact. The content of this report includes an overview of the community's key entities, their legal compliance, technical capabilities, reputation, and noteworthy claims.\n\n# Report Structure\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n4,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,\"**Analysis of Variance (ANOVA)**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**:  \nAnalysis of Variance (ANOVA) is a statistical method used to compare means among different groups to determine if at least one group mean is significantly different from the others. It is particularly useful in experimental design to analyze the effects of one or more factors on a response variable by partitioning the total variance into components attributable to different sources.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation**:  \nANOVA is based on the F-distribution and involves the calculation of the F-statistic, which is the ratio of the variance between the group means to the variance within the groups. The general model can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ Y_{ij} = \\mu + \\alpha_i + \\epsilon_{ij} \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( Y_{ij} \\) is the response variable,\n- \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean,\n- \\( \\alpha_i \\) is the effect of the \\( i \\)-th group,\n- \\( \\epsilon_{ij} \\) is the random error associated with the \\( j \\)-th observation in the \\( i \\)-th group.\n\n**Design Layout**:  \nANOVA can be applied in various experimental designs, including completely randomized designs, randomized block designs, and factorial designs. Each design structure allows for the assessment of different factors and their interactions.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**:  \n1. Independence of observations: Each group should be independent of the others.\n2. Normality: The data in each group should be approximately normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variances: The variances among the groups should be approximately equal (homoscedasticity).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**:  \nANOVA is applicable when comparing three or more group means. It is particularly useful when the researcher is interested in understanding the impact of categorical independent variables (factors) on a continuous dependent variable. It is selected when the assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variances are met.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**:  \n1. Formulate the null hypothesis (H0: all group means are equal) and the alternative hypothesis (H1: at least one group mean is different).\n2. Calculate the F-statistic using the ratio of the mean square between groups to the mean square within groups.\n3. Compare the calculated F-statistic to the critical value from the F-distribution table at a specified significance level (e.g., α = 0.05).\n4. If the F-statistic exceeds the critical value, reject the null hypothesis.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**:  \n- ANOVA can handle multiple groups simultaneously, reducing the risk of Type I error compared to multiple t-tests.\n- It provides a comprehensive analysis of variance, allowing for the assessment of the impact of multiple factors and their interactions.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**:  \n- ANOVA is sensitive to violations of its assumptions, particularly normality and homogeneity of variances.\n- It does not indicate which specific groups are different; post-hoc tests (e.g., Tukey's HSD) are required for pairwise comparisons.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**:  \n- Post-hoc tests (e.g., Tukey's HSD, Bonferroni correction) for pairwise comparisons after ANOVA.\n- Non-parametric alternatives such as the Kruskal-Wallis test when assumptions are violated.\n- Mixed-design ANOVA for studies involving both within-subjects and between-subjects factors.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**:  \nResearchers should ensure that the assumptions of ANOVA are met before applying the method. Data transformation techniques may be employed to address violations of normality or homogeneity. Additionally, sample sizes should be balanced across groups to enhance the robustness of the results.\n\n**Software Implementation**:  \nANOVA can be performed using various statistical software packages, including R, SAS, SPSS, and Python (using libraries such as SciPy and StatsModels). These tools provide functions to conduct ANOVA and generate relevant output, including F-statistics and p-values.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**:  \nANOVA is widely used in various fields, including agriculture (to compare crop yields under different treatments), medicine (to assess the effectiveness of different drugs), and engineering (to evaluate the impact of different manufacturing processes on product quality). Specific examples include experiments in vascular grafts, plasma etching, and filtration studies, where the significance of different factors on outcomes is analyzed.\",262\r\n239,F-VALUE,\"### F-Value\n\nThe F-Value is a critical statistical entity used in the context of Analysis of Variance (ANOVA). It serves as a ratio that compares the variance between groups to the variance within groups, thereby assisting in the determination of statistical significance regarding treatment effects.\n\n#### Definition and Purpose\nThe F-Value quantifies the degree to which the means of different groups differ relative to the variability observed within those groups. A higher F-Value indicates a greater disparity between group means compared to the variability within the groups, suggesting that the treatment effects may be significant.\n\n#### Mathematical Formulation\nMathematically, the F-Value is expressed as:\n\n\\[\nF = \\frac{\\text{Variance Between Groups}}{\\text{Variance Within Groups}} = \\frac{MS_{between}}{MS_{within}}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(MS_{between}\\) is the mean square between groups and \\(MS_{within}\\) is the mean square within groups. The mean squares are calculated as follows:\n\n- \\(MS_{between} = \\frac{SS_{between}}{df_{between}}\\)\n- \\(MS_{within} = \\frac{SS_{within}}{df_{within}}\\)\n\nHere, \\(SS\\) represents the sum of squares, and \\(df\\) denotes degrees of freedom.\n\n#### Design Layout\nThe F-Value is typically employed in the context of experimental designs that involve multiple groups or treatments, such as completely randomized designs, randomized block designs, and factorial designs. Each of these designs allows for the comparison of group means through the ANOVA framework.\n\n#### Assumptions Required for Validity\nFor the F-Value to be valid, several assumptions must be met:\n1. Independence of observations: The samples must be independent of one another.\n2. Normality: The data within each group should be approximately normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variances: The variances among the groups should be roughly equal.\n\n#### When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria\nThe F-Value is applicable in situations where researchers wish to compare the means of three or more groups to assess whether at least one group mean is significantly different from the others. It is particularly useful in experimental designs where multiple treatments or conditions are tested simultaneously.\n\n#### Analysis Procedure\nThe analysis procedure involving the F-Value typically includes:\n1. Formulating the null hypothesis (H0: all group means are equal) and the alternative hypothesis (H1: at least one group mean is different).\n2. Calculating the F-Value using the mean squares.\n3. Comparing the calculated F-Value to a critical value from the F-distribution table based on the appropriate degrees of freedom to determine statistical significance.\n\n#### Advantages and Strengths\n- The F-Value provides a straightforward method for comparing multiple group means simultaneously.\n- It helps in identifying significant treatment effects while controlling for Type I error across multiple comparisons.\n\n#### Limitations and Disadvantages\n- The F-Value is sensitive to violations of the assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variances.\n- It does not indicate which specific groups are different; post-hoc tests are required for that purpose.\n\n#### Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives\nThe F-Value is closely related to other statistical methods such as t-tests (for two groups) and can be extended to more complex designs like mixed models and repeated measures ANOVA. Alternatives to ANOVA that do not rely on the same assumptions include non-parametric tests like the Kruskal-Wallis test.\n\n#### Practical Implementation Considerations\nWhen implementing the F-Value in practice, researchers should ensure that the assumptions are checked and met. If assumptions are violated, data transformations or non-parametric methods may be considered.\n\n#### Software Implementation\nThe F-Value can be computed using various statistical software packages, including R, SAS, SPSS, and Python libraries such as SciPy and Statsmodels, which provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA.\n\n#### Common Applications or Examples\nThe F-Value is commonly used in various fields such as agriculture (to compare crop yields under different treatments), psychology (to assess the effects of different therapies), and manufacturing (to evaluate the impact of different production methods on product quality).\",2\r\n256,BLOCK TOTALS,\"**Entity: BLOCK TOTALS**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**  \nBlock totals refer to the sums of observations collected within each block in an experimental design. They serve a crucial role in the analysis of variance (ANOVA) by allowing researchers to assess the effects of treatments while controlling for variability associated with the blocking factors. By summarizing data within blocks, block totals facilitate the comparison of treatment effects across different experimental conditions.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**  \nIn a typical ANOVA framework, the total sum of squares can be partitioned into components attributable to treatments, blocks, and error. The block total for block \\( j \\) can be mathematically expressed as:\n\n\\[\nB_j = \\sum_{i=1}^{n_j} Y_{ij}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( B_j \\) is the block total for block \\( j \\), \\( Y_{ij} \\) represents the individual observations in block \\( j \\), and \\( n_j \\) is the number of observations in that block.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**  \nBlock totals are utilized in block designs, where experimental units are grouped into blocks that are similar to one another. Each block contains all treatment levels, ensuring that the effects of treatments can be evaluated while accounting for the variability between blocks. The layout typically involves a matrix where rows represent blocks and columns represent treatments.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**  \nThe validity of using block totals in analysis relies on several assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations within and across blocks.\n2. Homogeneity of variances across blocks (homoscedasticity).\n3. Normality of the distribution of residuals.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**  \nBlock totals are particularly applicable in situations where there is a known source of variability that can be controlled through blocking. Researchers should consider using block designs when:\n- There are identifiable factors that can be grouped (blocked) to reduce variability.\n- The number of treatments is manageable within each block.\n- The experimental units are heterogeneous, and blocking can help achieve more precise estimates of treatment effects.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**  \nThe analysis involving block totals typically follows these steps:\n1. Calculate block totals for each block.\n2. Compute the overall mean and treatment means.\n3. Perform ANOVA to partition the total variability into components attributable to treatments, blocks, and error.\n4. Conduct post-hoc tests if necessary to compare treatment means.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**  \n- Block totals enhance the precision of treatment effect estimates by controlling for variability.\n- They allow for a clearer interpretation of treatment effects by isolating the influence of blocking factors.\n- The use of block totals can lead to increased statistical power in detecting treatment differences.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**  \n- The effectiveness of blocking is contingent upon the correct identification of blocking factors; misidentification can lead to confounding.\n- Block designs may require more resources and careful planning, especially with a large number of treatments and blocks.\n- If the number of blocks is too small, the benefits of blocking may be diminished.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**  \nBlock totals are closely related to other designs such as randomized complete block designs (RCBD) and Latin square designs. Alternatives include factorial designs and completely randomized designs, which may be more suitable in the absence of identifiable blocking factors.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**  \nWhen implementing block designs, researchers should ensure that:\n- Blocks are formed based on relevant characteristics that influence the response variable.\n- The number of treatments and blocks is balanced to avoid complications in analysis.\n- Data collection methods are consistent across blocks to maintain the integrity of block totals.\n\n**Software Implementation:**  \nMany statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and SPSS, provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA that can incorporate block totals. Users can specify block factors in their models to facilitate the analysis of variance.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**  \nBlock totals are commonly used in agricultural experiments, clinical trials, and industrial quality control studies, where controlling for environmental or operational variability is essential for accurate treatment effect estimation. For instance, in agricultural research, different plots of land may serve as blocks to account for soil variability when testing the effects of different fertilizers.\",2\r\n951,TENSILE STRENGTH EXPERIMENT,,2\r\n953,MULTIPLE COMPARISON METHODS,Multiple comparison methods are statistical techniques used to compare multiple treatment means to identify which specific means differ from each other.,2\r\n1042,KRUSKAL-WALLIS TEST,\"### Kruskal-Wallis Test\n\nThe Kruskal-Wallis test is a non-parametric statistical method used to determine whether there are statistically significant differences between the medians of two or more independent groups. It serves as an alternative to the F-test in analysis of variance (ANOVA) when the assumptions of ANOVA are not met, particularly when the data do not follow a normal distribution.\n\n#### Mathematical Formulation\nThe Kruskal-Wallis test ranks all the data from all groups together and then compares the sum of ranks for each group. The test statistic \\( H \\) is calculated using the formula:\n\n\\[\nH = \\frac{12}{N(N + 1)} \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\frac{R_i^2}{n_i} - 3(N + 1)\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( N \\) is the total number of observations,\n- \\( k \\) is the number of groups,\n- \\( R_i \\) is the sum of ranks for group \\( i \\),\n- \\( n_i \\) is the number of observations in group \\( i \\).\n\n#### Design Layout\nThe Kruskal-Wallis test does not require a specific experimental design layout, as it can be applied to any situation where independent samples are collected. However, it is typically used in scenarios where there are three or more groups being compared.\n\n#### Assumptions\nThe validity of the Kruskal-Wallis test relies on several assumptions:\n1. The samples are independent.\n2. The dependent variable is ordinal or continuous.\n3. The distributions of the groups have the same shape (homogeneity of variance).\n\n#### When to Use / Applicability\nThe Kruskal-Wallis test is applicable when:\n- The sample sizes are small or unequal.\n- The data do not meet the assumptions of normality required for ANOVA.\n- The researcher is interested in comparing the medians of multiple groups.\n\n#### Analysis Procedure\nTo conduct the Kruskal-Wallis test, the following steps are typically followed:\n1. Rank all the data from all groups together.\n2. Calculate the test statistic \\( H \\) using the formula provided.\n3. Compare the calculated \\( H \\) value to the critical value from the chi-squared distribution with \\( k-1 \\) degrees of freedom to determine significance.\n\n#### Advantages and Strengths\n- The Kruskal-Wallis test is robust to violations of normality and is suitable for ordinal data.\n- It can handle unequal sample sizes and is less sensitive to outliers compared to parametric tests.\n\n#### Limitations and Disadvantages\n- The test does not indicate which specific groups are different; post-hoc tests are required for pairwise comparisons.\n- It assumes that the distributions of the groups are similar in shape, which may not always be the case.\n\n#### Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives\nThe Kruskal-Wallis test is related to other non-parametric tests such as the Mann-Whitney U test (for two groups) and the Friedman test (for repeated measures). Alternatives include the one-way ANOVA when the assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variance are satisfied.\n\n#### Practical Implementation Considerations\nWhen implementing the Kruskal-Wallis test, researchers should ensure that the data is appropriately ranked and that the assumptions are checked. It is also important to consider the need for post-hoc analysis if significant differences are found.\n\n#### Software Implementation\nThe Kruskal-Wallis test can be easily performed using statistical software such as R, Python (SciPy library), SPSS, and SAS, which provide built-in functions for conducting the test and generating results.\n\n#### Common Applications or Examples\nThe Kruskal-Wallis test is commonly used in various fields, including psychology, medicine, and social sciences, to compare the effectiveness of different treatments, assess differences in survey responses across groups, or evaluate performance metrics across different conditions.\",2\r\n53,PARTIAL CONFOUNDING,\"**Partial Confounding**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**: Partial confounding is a statistical phenomenon that occurs in experimental design and analysis when a factor is not completely confounded across all replicates. This allows for the estimation of effects from multiple sources, even when some level of confounding exists. The primary purpose of recognizing and utilizing partial confounding is to enhance the accuracy of treatment comparisons while still accounting for variability introduced by other factors.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**: In the context of partial confounding, the model can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y_{ijk} = \\mu + \\alpha_i + \\beta_j + \\epsilon_{ijk} \\]\nwhere \\( Y_{ijk} \\) is the response variable, \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean, \\( \\alpha_i \\) represents the effect of the \\( i \\)-th treatment, \\( \\beta_j \\) denotes the block effect, and \\( \\epsilon_{ijk} \\) is the random error term. The structure allows for the estimation of treatment effects while acknowledging the presence of block effects.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**: In experimental designs employing partial confounding, treatment combinations are strategically mixed with block effects. This design structure typically involves a factorial arrangement where treatments are assigned to experimental units in a way that some treatment combinations share common blocks, thus allowing for the recovery of information about confounded effects.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**: The validity of partial confounding relies on several assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: The experimental units must be independent.\n2. Normality: The residuals should be normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variance: The variance among treatment groups should be approximately equal.\n4. Correct specification of the model: The model must accurately reflect the underlying relationships among factors.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**: Partial confounding is particularly useful in situations where complete randomization is not feasible, or when resources are limited. It is applicable in complex experimental designs where multiple factors are being studied, and some factors may be difficult to isolate. Researchers should consider using partial confounding when they anticipate that certain effects can be estimated despite the presence of confounding.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**: The analysis of data from experiments with partial confounding typically involves the use of Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) techniques. The analysis aims to partition the total variability into components attributable to the treatment effects, block effects, and error. The significance of treatment effects can be assessed using F-tests.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**: The strengths of partial confounding include:\n- Improved accuracy in estimating treatment effects when complete confounding is unavoidable.\n- The ability to extract useful information from confounded data, which can lead to more informed decision-making.\n- Flexibility in experimental design, allowing for the incorporation of block effects without sacrificing the ability to analyze treatment effects.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**: The limitations of partial confounding include:\n- Potential for biased estimates if the confounding is not properly accounted for.\n- Complexity in the analysis and interpretation of results, particularly when multiple factors are involved.\n- The risk of overfitting the model if too many confounding factors are included.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**: Related designs include full confounding, where factors are completely confounded, and factorial designs that do not incorporate blocking. Alternatives to partial confounding may involve using more sophisticated statistical techniques such as mixed models or Bayesian approaches to account for confounding.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**: When implementing partial confounding, researchers should carefully plan the experimental layout to ensure that the confounding structure is well understood. It is crucial to document the design and analysis process thoroughly to facilitate interpretation and replication of results.\n\n**Software Implementation**: While specific software implementations are not mentioned, common statistical software packages such as R, SAS, and SPSS can be utilized to perform ANOVA and other analyses relevant to partial confounding. These tools often provide functions for handling mixed models and estimating effects in the presence of confounding.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**: Partial confounding is commonly applied in agricultural experiments, clinical trials, and industrial process studies where multiple factors influence the outcome. For instance, in agricultural research, partial confounding may be used to evaluate the effects of different fertilizers while accounting for variability due to soil type or environmental conditions.\",1\r\n54,BALANCED PARTIALLY CONFOUNDED DESIGN,A balanced partially confounded design is an experimental design where all effects of a certain order are confounded with block differences in equal numbers of replicates.,1\r\n55,UNBALANCED PARTIALLY CONFOUNDED DESIGN,An unbalanced partially confounded design is an experimental design where effects of a certain order are confounded an unequal number of times in a design.,1\r\n81,CONFOUNDED FACTOR C,\"Confounded Factor C is a statistical term referring to a variable that is not accounted for in an analysis, which can affect the results.\",1\r\n84,ARITHMETIC MEAN,Arithmetic Mean is a measure of central tendency calculated by summing a set of values and dividing by the number of values.,1\r\n150,GAUSS MARKOFF THEOREM,\"### Gauss-Markov Theorem\n\nThe Gauss-Markov theorem is a fundamental result in the field of statistics, particularly in the context of linear regression and the design of experiments. It establishes the conditions under which the ordinary least squares (OLS) estimator is the best linear unbiased estimator (BLUE) of the parameters in a linear model.\n\n#### Definition and Purpose\nThe Gauss-Markov theorem asserts that if a linear parametric function is estimable, then the linear estimator derived from the ordinary least squares method is the best linear unbiased estimator. Specifically, it states that for a linear parametric function \\( L' \\beta \\), where \\( L \\) is a known matrix and \\( \\beta \\) is the vector of parameters, the estimator \\( L' \\hat{\\beta} \\) is the BLUE of \\( L' \\beta \\) with the minimum variance among all linear unbiased estimators.\n\n#### Mathematical Formulation\nThe theorem can be mathematically expressed as follows:\n- Let \\( Y \\) be the vector of observations, \\( X \\) the design matrix, and \\( \\beta \\) the vector of parameters. The OLS estimator is given by:\n  \\[\n  \\hat{\\beta} = (X'X)^{-1}X'Y\n  \\]\n- The linear estimator \\( L' \\hat{\\beta} \\) is then defined, and the theorem guarantees that it has the minimum variance among all linear unbiased estimators under the conditions specified.\n\n#### Assumptions Required for Validity\nThe Gauss-Markov theorem relies on several key assumptions:\n1. **Linearity**: The relationship between the independent variables and the dependent variable is linear.\n2. **Independence**: The observations are independent of each other.\n3. **Homoscedasticity**: The variance of the errors is constant across all levels of the independent variables.\n4. **No perfect multicollinearity**: The independent variables are not perfectly correlated.\n\n#### When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria\nThe Gauss-Markov theorem is applicable in situations where one is estimating parameters in a linear regression model. It is particularly useful when the assumptions of linearity, independence, and homoscedasticity are met. Researchers should consider using OLS estimators when they require unbiased estimates with minimum variance.\n\n#### Analysis Procedure\nTo apply the Gauss-Markov theorem in practice, one typically follows these steps:\n1. Specify the linear model.\n2. Collect data and construct the design matrix \\( X \\).\n3. Compute the OLS estimator \\( \\hat{\\beta} \\).\n4. Verify the assumptions of the model.\n5. Use the estimator \\( L' \\hat{\\beta} \\) for inference.\n\n#### Advantages and Strengths\n- The OLS estimator is unbiased and has the lowest variance among all linear unbiased estimators, making it efficient.\n- It provides a straightforward method for estimating parameters in linear models.\n\n#### Limitations and Disadvantages\n- The theorem's validity is contingent upon the assumptions being met; violations can lead to biased or inefficient estimates.\n- It does not apply to non-linear models or when the assumptions of independence and homoscedasticity are violated.\n\n#### Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives\nThe Gauss-Markov theorem is closely related to other statistical methods, including:\n- Generalized least squares (GLS), which can be used when the assumptions of the Gauss-Markov theorem are violated.\n- Non-linear regression models, which require different estimation techniques.\n\n#### Practical Implementation Considerations\nWhen implementing the Gauss-Markov theorem, practitioners should ensure that the assumptions are thoroughly checked, possibly using diagnostic plots and tests for homoscedasticity and independence. \n\n#### Software Implementation\nMany statistical software packages, such as R, Python (with libraries like statsmodels), and SAS, provide built-in functions to perform OLS regression and check the assumptions of the Gauss-Markov theorem.\n\n#### Common Applications or Examples\nThe Gauss-Markov theorem is widely used in various fields, including economics, social sciences, and biomedical research, where linear regression models are employed to analyze relationships between variables and make predictions based on empirical data.\",1\r\n203,POPULATION,,1\r\n198,CRITICAL DIFFERENCE,Critical difference is a calculated value used to compare observed differences in means to determine statistical significance.,1\r\n202,POPULATION MEANS,Population means refer to the average values of the groups being compared in the analysis.,1\r\n258,MINIMIZING,\"**Entity: Minimizing**\n\nMinimizing refers to the process of finding the lowest value of a function, which is a fundamental concept in various statistical methods and optimization problems. The primary purpose of minimizing is to reduce error or cost in statistical models, thereby enhancing the accuracy and efficiency of predictions or estimations.\n\nMathematically, minimizing can be expressed through optimization problems where the objective function \\( f(x) \\) is to be minimized. This can be represented as:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Minimize } f(x) \\quad \\text{subject to } g_i(x) \\leq 0, \\quad h_j(x) = 0\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( g_i \\) and \\( h_j \\) are constraints on the variables \\( x \\). The solution to this problem yields the optimal values of \\( x \\) that minimize \\( f(x) \\).\n\nIn the context of experimental designs, minimizing is often applied to reduce the variance of estimates or to optimize the allocation of resources in experiments. This can involve minimizing the sum of squared errors in regression models or minimizing the total cost in resource allocation problems.\n\nThe assumptions required for the validity of minimizing techniques typically include the continuity and differentiability of the objective function, as well as the presence of a feasible region defined by constraints. Additionally, the function should ideally exhibit convexity to ensure that any local minimum is also a global minimum.\n\nMinimizing is applicable in various scenarios, particularly when the goal is to optimize performance, reduce costs, or enhance the precision of statistical estimates. Selection criteria for employing minimizing techniques often depend on the nature of the problem, the characteristics of the data, and the specific objectives of the analysis.\n\nThe analysis procedure for minimizing generally involves the use of optimization algorithms, such as gradient descent, Newton's method, or other numerical techniques, to iteratively approach the minimum value of the objective function. These methods may require the computation of derivatives and can be implemented using various software tools.\n\nThe advantages of minimizing include its ability to provide optimal solutions to complex problems, enhance model performance, and facilitate decision-making processes. It is a powerful tool in both theoretical and applied statistics, allowing researchers to derive meaningful insights from data.\n\nHowever, minimizing also has limitations. It can be sensitive to the choice of initial values, may converge to local minima in non-convex problems, and can require significant computational resources for large datasets or complex models. Additionally, the presence of noise in data can complicate the minimization process.\n\nRelated designs and methods include various optimization techniques such as linear programming, nonlinear programming, and other statistical methods that focus on minimizing error or cost. Alternatives may involve heuristic approaches or simulation-based optimization when traditional methods are not feasible.\n\nPractical implementation considerations for minimizing involve careful selection of optimization algorithms based on the problem structure, ensuring that assumptions are met, and validating results through sensitivity analysis. Software implementations for minimizing can be found in statistical packages such as R, Python (using libraries like SciPy), and specialized optimization software like MATLAB or GAMS.\n\nCommon applications of minimizing include regression analysis, resource allocation in experimental designs, machine learning model training, and any scenario where optimization of a function is required to achieve desired outcomes.\",1\r\n264,SSAB,Sum of Squares for Interaction AB (SSAB) measures the variation explained by the interaction between two factors in ANOVA.,1\r\n267,TUKEY’S TEST,Tukey’s test is a statistical test used for comparing means among groups to determine if they are significantly different.,1\r\n269,NONADDITIVITY,Nonadditivity refers to a situation in statistical models where the effect of one variable is not simply additive to the effects of other variables.,1\r\n271,FISHER-BEHREN'S PROBLEM,Fisher-Behrens problem refers to the statistical problem of testing the equality of variances from two independent samples.,1\r\n282,ONE-WAY ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,One-way analysis of variance is a statistical technique used to compare the means of three or more independent groups to see if at least one mean is different.,1\r\n283,LEAST SQUARES PRINCIPLE,\"The least squares principle is a method used in regression analysis to minimize the sum of the squares of the residuals, which are the differences between observed and predicted values.\",1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n22,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,LINEAR ALGEBRA,Analysis of Variance utilizes concepts from Linear Algebra to analyze the variance among group means.,264\r\n249,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,F-VALUE,\"### Comprehensive Summary of Analysis of Variance and F-Value\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nAnalysis of Variance (ANOVA) is a statistical method used to determine whether there are statistically significant differences between the means of three or more independent groups. The primary purpose of ANOVA is to test hypotheses regarding treatment effects by comparing the variances within groups to the variances between groups. The F-value is a key statistic derived from ANOVA, which quantifies the ratio of the variance explained by the model to the variance unexplained (error variance).\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nThe F-value is calculated using the formula:\n\n\\[\nF = \\frac{\\text{MS}_{\\text{between}}}{\\text{MS}_{\\text{within}}}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\text{MS}_{\\text{between}}\\) is the mean square between groups (variance due to the treatment) and \\(\\text{MS}_{\\text{within}}\\) is the mean square within groups (variance due to error). The mean squares are calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\text{MS}_{\\text{between}} = \\frac{SS_{\\text{between}}}{df_{\\text{between}}}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{MS}_{\\text{within}} = \\frac{SS_{\\text{within}}}{df_{\\text{within}}}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(SS\\) represents the sum of squares and \\(df\\) represents degrees of freedom.\n\n**Design Layout:**\nANOVA can be applied in various experimental designs, including completely randomized designs, randomized block designs, and factorial designs. Each design structure allows for the assessment of treatment effects while controlling for variability.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\n1. Independence of observations: Each group should consist of independent samples.\n2. Normality: The data in each group should be approximately normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variances: The variances among the groups should be approximately equal (homoscedasticity).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nANOVA is applicable when comparing the means of three or more groups. It is particularly useful when the researcher is interested in understanding the effect of one or more categorical independent variables (factors) on a continuous dependent variable. Selection criteria include the number of groups, the design of the experiment, and the nature of the data.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis involves the following steps:\n1. Formulate the null hypothesis (no difference in group means) and the alternative hypothesis (at least one group mean is different).\n2. Calculate the F-value using the mean squares.\n3. Compare the calculated F-value to the critical F-value from the F-distribution table at a specified significance level (e.g., α = 0.05).\n4. If the calculated F-value exceeds the critical value, reject the null hypothesis.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- ANOVA allows for the comparison of multiple groups simultaneously, reducing the risk of Type I error associated with multiple t-tests.\n- It provides a robust framework for analyzing experimental data with multiple factors and interactions.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- ANOVA is sensitive to violations of its assumptions, particularly normality and homogeneity of variances.\n- It does not indicate which specific groups are different; post-hoc tests are required for that purpose.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated methods include t-tests for two-group comparisons, MANOVA for multiple dependent variables, and regression analysis for continuous predictors. Alternatives to ANOVA when assumptions are violated include non-parametric tests such as the Kruskal-Wallis test.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nResearchers should ensure that the assumptions of ANOVA are met before conducting the analysis. If assumptions are violated, data transformation or alternative methods should be considered. Sample size should also be adequate to ensure sufficient power for detecting differences.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nANOVA can be performed using various statistical software packages, including R, SAS, SPSS, and Python (using libraries such as SciPy and StatsModels). These tools provide functions to conduct ANOVA and calculate F-values easily.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nANOVA is widely used in fields such as agriculture (to compare crop yields under different treatments), psychology (to assess the effects of different therapies), and medicine (to evaluate the efficacy of different drugs). It is a fundamental tool in experimental design and analysis across various disciplines.\",264\r\n310,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,BLOCK TOTALS,Block Totals are used in Analysis of Variance to assess the effects of treatments within blocks.,264\r\n1080,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,TENSILE STRENGTH EXPERIMENT,Analysis of Variance is applied to the tensile strength experiment to assess differences in means,264\r\n1086,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,MULTIPLE COMPARISON METHODS,Multiple comparison methods are often employed following ANOVA to further investigate which treatment means are significantly different.,264\r\n1267,KRUSKAL-WALLIS TEST,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,\"The Kruskal-Wallis test serves as a nonparametric alternative to the analysis of variance, particularly when normality assumptions are not met.\",264\r\n48,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,PARTIAL CONFOUNDING,\"### Comprehensive Summary of Analysis of Variance and Partial Confounding\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nAnalysis of Variance (ANOVA) is a statistical method used to determine whether there are statistically significant differences between the means of three or more independent groups. It helps in assessing the impact of one or more factors by comparing the variance within groups to the variance between groups. Partial confounding, on the other hand, refers to a situation in experimental design where the effects of one or more factors are not completely separable from the effects of other factors, leading to a situation where the true effect of a factor may be obscured or misestimated.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nThe general model for ANOVA can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y_{ij} = \\mu + \\alpha_i + \\epsilon_{ij} \\]\nwhere:\n- \\( Y_{ij} \\) is the response variable,\n- \\( \\mu \\) is the overall mean,\n- \\( \\alpha_i \\) is the effect of the \\( i \\)-th treatment (factor level),\n- \\( \\epsilon_{ij} \\) is the random error associated with the \\( j \\)-th observation in the \\( i \\)-th group.\n\nIn the context of partial confounding, the model may include additional terms to account for the confounding factors, complicating the interpretation of the treatment effects.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nANOVA can be applied in various experimental designs, including completely randomized designs, randomized block designs, and factorial designs. The layout typically involves grouping experimental units into different treatment levels, with each group receiving a different treatment or condition.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of ANOVA relies on several key assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations.\n2. Normality of the response variable within each group.\n3. Homogeneity of variances across groups (equal variances).\n\nFor partial confounding, additional considerations may include the need to identify and measure potential confounding factors to mitigate their effects.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nANOVA is applicable when comparing means across multiple groups, particularly when the researcher is interested in understanding the influence of categorical independent variables on a continuous dependent variable. Partial confounding is particularly relevant in complex experimental designs where multiple factors are involved, and the researcher must be cautious about interpreting the results due to potential overlaps in factor effects.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis procedure for ANOVA typically involves:\n1. Calculating group means and overall mean.\n2. Computing the sum of squares for between-group and within-group variations.\n3. Determining the F-statistic to test the null hypothesis that all group means are equal.\n4. Conducting post-hoc tests if the null hypothesis is rejected to identify which specific groups differ.\n\nIn cases of partial confounding, additional statistical techniques, such as regression analysis or stratification, may be employed to adjust for confounding factors.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nANOVA is powerful for comparing multiple groups simultaneously, reducing the risk of Type I error compared to multiple t-tests. It provides a clear framework for hypothesis testing and can be extended to more complex designs, such as factorial ANOVA. Partial confounding, when recognized and addressed, allows for a more nuanced understanding of the data and can lead to more accurate conclusions.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nANOVA is sensitive to violations of its assumptions, particularly normality and homogeneity of variances. If these assumptions are not met, the results may be misleading. Partial confounding can complicate the interpretation of results, making it difficult to ascertain the true effects of the factors involved.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated methods include t-tests for two-group comparisons, mixed-effects models for hierarchical data, and regression analysis for continuous predictors. Alternatives to ANOVA include non-parametric tests such as the Kruskal-Wallis test when assumptions are violated.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing ANOVA, researchers should ensure proper randomization and replication to enhance the reliability of results. In the presence of partial confounding, careful design and measurement of confounding variables are crucial to mitigate their impact.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nANOVA can be performed using various statistical software packages, including R, SAS, SPSS, and Python's SciPy and Statsmodels libraries. These tools provide functions for conducting ANOVA and can handle complex designs, including those with confounding factors.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nANOVA is widely used in fields such as agriculture (to compare crop yields under different treatments), medicine (to evaluate the effectiveness of different drugs), and social sciences (to assess differences in survey responses across demographic groups). Partial confounding is often encountered in observational studies where multiple factors may influence the outcome, necessitating careful design and analysis to draw valid conclusions.\",263\r\n49,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,BALANCED PARTIALLY CONFOUNDED DESIGN,Balanced partially confounded design is a specific type of design discussed in the context of Analysis of Variance,263\r\n50,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,UNBALANCED PARTIALLY CONFOUNDED DESIGN,Unbalanced partially confounded design is another type of design discussed in the context of Analysis of Variance,263\r\n76,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,CONFOUNDED FACTOR C,\"Confounded Factor C is a variable that can affect the results in the analysis of variance, indicating a relationship between them.\",263\r\n80,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,ARITHMETIC MEAN,Arithmetic Mean is often used in analysis of variance to summarize data points and compare group means.,263\r\n154,GAUSS MARKOFF THEOREM,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,The Gauss Markoff theorem provides foundational principles that are applied in the Analysis of Variance methodology.,263\r\n191,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,POPULATION,Analysis of Variance is used to analyze differences among population means,263\r\n192,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,CRITICAL DIFFERENCE,Critical difference is a key component in determining the results of the Analysis of Variance,263\r\n195,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,POPULATION MEANS,Population means are the focus of the Analysis of Variance to determine if they are equal or not,263\r\n239,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,MINIMIZING,Minimizing is a crucial step in the process of conducting Analysis of Variance,263\r\n247,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,SSAB,SSAB is used in the analysis of variance to quantify the interaction effect between Factors A and B.,263\r\n251,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,TUKEY’S TEST,Tukey’s test is a method used within the framework of Analysis of Variance to compare group means.,263\r\n260,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,NONADDITIVITY,Analysis of Variance includes considerations for nonadditivity in its statistical models.,263\r\n264,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,FISHER-BEHREN'S PROBLEM,Fisher-Behrens problem is a specific case addressed within the broader context of Analysis of Variance.,263\r\n277,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,ONE-WAY ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,One-way analysis of variance is a specific type of analysis of variance used for comparing means across one factor.,263\r\n279,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,LEAST SQUARES PRINCIPLE,The least squares principle is used in the context of the Analysis of Variance to estimate treatment effects.,263\r\n\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\nOutput:", "role": "user"}], "parameters": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "frequency_penalty": 0.0, "max_tokens": 4000, "n": 1, "presence_penalty": 0.0, "temperature": 0.0, "top_p": 1.0, "response_format": {"type": "json_object"}}}, "key": "chat_create_community_report_96a3b82280d458805f4f02e70d796672669cdcaca32a936a626fcb41ac71ebc4_v2"}